312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 198 0 R
/T (    For this project, our team chose to code the robot using a State Machine structure for two main reasons. )
/E (    For this project, our team chose to code the robot using a State Machine structure for two main reasons. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 198 0 R
/T (    The first reason was that the problem which the system solves can best be broken down into states. One way to think about programming this robot is to imagine it as an advanced vending machine. It must locate the desired item, collect the item, bring it to the deposit zone, and deposit the item. These are repeatable steps which will all be executed in the same order \(with minor exceptions\) every time the system is used, making a state based system an obvious choice. )
/E (    The first reason was that the problem which the system solves can best be broken down into states. One way to think about programming this robot is to imagine it as an advanced vending machine. It must locate the desired item, collect the item, bring it to the deposit zone, and deposit the item. These are repeatable steps which will all be executed in the same order \(with minor exceptions\) every time the system is used, making a state based system an obvious choice. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 198 0 R
/T (   The other reason was for troubleshooting simplicity. As previously stated, our group had the most room to grow in the field of programming, however we had limited time to implement the project. Not wanting to dedicate too much time to troubleshooting issues, we looked for a structure that would allow for easy troubleshooting. A state based system is extremely easy to troubleshoot because each state can be altered individually, meaning issues can be easily isolated and flaws with an individual state will typically not break the whole system. )
/E (   The other reason was for troubleshooting simplicity. As previously stated, our group had the most room to grow in the field of programming, however we had limited time to implement the project. Not wanting to dedicate too much time to troubleshooting issues, we looked for a structure that would allow for easy troubleshooting. A state based system is extremely easy to troubleshoot because each state can be altered individually, meaning issues can be easily isolated and flaws with an individual state will typically not break the whole system. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
